Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on the Work of Reporting and Approving the Implementation of Judicial Reform Matters of the People's Courts

Fa Fa [2023] No. 10

To the High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region; all units of this Court; and the Discipline Inspection and Supervision Group stationed at this Court:

The Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on the Work of Reporting and Approving the Implementation of Judicial Reform Matters of the People's Courts are hereby issued to you. Please carefully implement them in light of actual circumstances. If any issues arise during implementation, promptly report them to the Supreme People's Court.

June 8, 2023

Supreme People's Court

Provisions on the Work of Reporting and Approving the Implementation of Judicial Reform Matters of the People's Courts

To thoroughly implement the guiding principles of the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China, fully implement Xi Jinping Thought on the Rule of Law, ensure the effective implementation of various judicial reform tasks deployed by the Central Committee, and guarantee that the judicial reform of the people's courts is conducted in the correct direction, with legal basis, and in a stable and orderly manner, these provisions are hereby formulated for the work of reporting and approving the implementation of judicial reform matters of the people's courts.

1. In advancing judicial reform, the people's courts shall adhere to the guidance of Xi Jinping Thought on the Rule of Law. On the basis of fully understanding the situation and accurately identifying problems, they shall fully solicit opinions and suggestions from all relevant parties, conduct in-depth investigation and research, strengthen studies on necessity and feasibility, enhance and optimize the prediction of reform effects, impact assessment, and reporting and approval procedures, simultaneously improve supporting mechanisms, optimize the functions of the research office, and ensure that the judicial reform work of the people's courts consistently adheres to the centralized and unified leadership of the Party, consistently adheres to a people-centered approach, consistently adheres to a problem-oriented approach, consistently reflects systematic concepts and scientific methods, consistently implements tasks with the spirit of driving nails, and consistently integrates the requirements of comprehensive and strict governance and management of the courts throughout all processes and links of the reform.

2. Where any local people's court at any level or a special people's court intends to advance a judicial reform matter that falls under any of the following circumstances, it shall report it level by level to the Supreme People's Court for review and approval before implementation:

(1) New situations or new problems are encountered, and the laws, regulations, or judicial interpretations do not contain provisions or the provisions are unclear, making it necessary to pilot first;

(2) Innovative measures may conflict with existing provisions and require authorization before implementation;

(3) Adjustments to litigation mechanisms or judicial policies are involved;

(4) Major adjustments to policies concerning the functions, organizational establishment, team building, or occupational safeguards of the people's courts are involved;

(5) Division of powers and responsibilities with other state organs is involved;

(6) Other circumstances that may have a significant social impact.

Where, in accordance with the overall reform deployment of the locality, a reform task undertaken by a people's court falls under any of the above circumstances, the relevant people's court shall report it level by level to the Supreme People's Court for review and approval before implementation.

For matters mentioned above that involve major institutional, policy, or controversial issues, they shall be submitted to the Party Leadership Group of the Supreme People's Court for discussion and decision in accordance with relevant provisions, or a request for instructions shall be made to the Central Committee of the Party or the Central Political and Legal Affairs Commission.

3. For matters requiring reporting and approval under these Provisions, the relevant people's court shall form a written request for instructions and report it level by level to the Supreme People's Court. A request for instructions shall address one matter at a time, and no matters requesting instructions shall be inserted into non-instructional official documents.

The written request for instructions shall specify the main content, expected objectives of the relevant reform matter, as well as the policy support and supporting measures required from the Supreme People's Court, the higher people's court, or other state organs, and shall be accompanied by materials such as a feasibility study report, a preliminary reform plan, and relevant normative documents. The feasibility study report shall include the following:

(1) The basic background, policy basis, legal basis, and scope of application for the proposed reform matter;

(2) The necessity argumentation and the status of solicitation of opinions for the relevant reform matter;

(3) Favorable conditions, unfavorable factors, and risk assessment for advancing the reform;

(4) Opinions and suggestions from local Party and government organs and relevant political and legal units;

(5) Other important issues that need to be explained.

The high people's court and the intermediate people's court shall carefully study the judicial reform matters submitted by the people's courts within their jurisdiction and put forward preliminary processing suggestions, and shall not merely forward the original request for instructions to the court at the next higher level.

4. Requests for instructions on judicial reform matters submitted by each high people's court shall be uniformly received, registered, reviewed, approved, and responded to by the Research Office of the Supreme People's Court. If, after review, it is determined that a matter does not fall within the scope of matters requiring reporting and approval, the Research Office shall, after approval by the responsible leader of the Research Office and the leading official in charge of the court, return it within fifteen days and state the reasons; if it does fall within the scope, the reviewing department shall be determined based on the internal functional division of the Supreme People's Court, and the matter shall be distributed to the relevant departments for review after approval by the leading official in charge of the Research Office.

5. The reviewing department shall focus on reviewing the following:

(1) Whether it conforms to the reform spirit of the Central Committee;

(2) Whether it conforms to the provisions of the Constitution, laws, regulations, and judicial interpretations;

(3) Whether it is a matter that should be requested for instructions from the Central Committee of the Party or the Central Political and Legal Affairs Commission;

(4) Whether it involves matters requiring communication and coordination with other central state organs;

(5) Whether it requires authorization from the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress to adjust the application of relevant laws;

(6) Whether it adheres to a problem-oriented approach and is conducive to solving the pressing difficulties and concerns of the people or practical problems restricting the modernization of trial work;

(7) Whether it is scientific, necessary, feasible, and appropriate;

(8) Possible effects, risks, and countermeasures;

(9) Other content that should be reviewed.

6. The reviewing department shall issue a written review opinion within thirty days from the date of receiving the relevant materials. If other central state organs or multiple departments within the court are involved, opinions may first be solicited from the relevant departments. The written review opinion shall be sent to the Research Office after approval by the leading official in charge of the department.

7. The reviewing department shall, depending on the specific circumstances, propose processing suggestions in the written review opinion:

(1) If the content meets the requirements of these Provisions, consent to advance the relevant judicial reform matter;

(2) If it meets the requirements but there are suggestions for modification, propose specific modifications and reasons, and request supplementary materials if necessary;

(3) If the basis for advancing the relevant judicial reform matter is insufficient, the conditions are not mature, it exceeds judicial functions, there are major risks, or it falls within the exclusive reform authority of the Supreme People's Court, return it and state the reasons.

If there are major disagreements among the departments within the court regarding the judicial reform matter, the leading official in charge of the reviewing department shall take the lead in organizing coordination. If necessary, it is recommended to submit the matter to the Party Leadership Group of the court for discussion and decision.

8. The Research Office shall review the review opinion, draft a formal reply letter. If it is necessary to submit the matter to the Party Leadership Group of the Supreme People's Court for discussion and decision or to request instructions from the Central Committee of the Party or the Central Political and Legal Affairs Commission, the formal reply letter shall be drafted based on the decision of the Party Leadership Group or the approval of the Central Committee of the Party or the Central Political and Legal Affairs Commission. The reply letter, after being examined and signed by the President, shall be sent by the General Office to the relevant high people's court in the form of a "Fa Ban Han," and the high people's court shall promptly forward it to the relevant people's court.

For reform matters approved by the Supreme People's Court, the relevant high people's court shall, within thirty days after the start of the reform, submit the formal reform plan, the initiation status, and relevant normative documents to the Research Office of the Supreme People's Court for record, and shall submit a report on the progress of the reform once every six months.

9. If a judicial reform matter of a people's court requires adjustment to the application of current laws, it shall be implemented by the Supreme People's Court organizing a pilot project after approval by the Central Committee of the Party and authorization by the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress.

The duration of a judicial reform pilot project approved by the Supreme People's Court shall generally be one year and shall not exceed two years in principle. The Research Office of the Supreme People's Court shall establish a ledger for judicial reform pilots, and summarize and clear them every six months. The ledger for judicial reform pilots shall be managed on an account basis, cleared regularly, and accepted upon expiry. The reviewing department, together with the Research Office, shall be responsible for guidance and acceptance work. For pilots for which the Central Committee has already launched relevant judicial reform measures, or for which relevant laws have been formulated or amended during the pilot period, the pilot shall be declared concluded by notice; for pilots with slow progress, poor organization, or violations of laws or regulations, timely warnings shall be issued, rectification urged, and if necessary, early termination and accountability in accordance with discipline measures shall be taken; for pilots that need to be integrated, timely merger shall be declared; for pilots that have expired, timely summary and closure shall be conducted.

10. For judicial reform matters approved by the Supreme People's Court, the reviewing department, together with the relevant high people's court, shall conduct business guidance, mid-term evaluation, summary acceptance, and replication and promotion work. For matters proven effective and replicable through practice, timely research, demonstration, and the formulation of promotion plans shall be conducted; for matters that are not effectively implemented or have weak pertinence or operability, timely account rectification shall be carried out; for matters that prove ineffective in actual testing, timely adjustment of plans or a request to stop shall be made.

11. The Supreme People's Court shall strengthen supervision and guidance over the reporting and approval work of judicial reform matters through various forms such as research, inspections, and circulars. If any of the following circumstances exist and cause serious consequences, the responsible leading officials and staff of the relevant people's courts shall be held accountable in accordance with laws and regulations:

(1) Violation of these Provisions by failing to request instructions or report on judicial reform matters that should have been requested or reported;

(2) Unilaterally launching judicial reform measures that should have been reviewed and approved by the Supreme People's Court;

(3) Lacking a sense of responsibility, shirking responsibilities, shifting contradictions, or acting passively;

(4) Engaging in formalism or bureaucracy, with false or inaccurate content or information in requests or reports.

12. For judicial reform matters that are deployed by the relevant national plans, programs, or key points under the leadership of the Supreme People's Court, after a written request for instructions to the Central Political and Legal Affairs Commission and obtaining consent, they shall be reported in accordance with procedures to the Central Committee of the Party, the Central Commission for Comprehensively Deepening Reforms, or the Central Commission for Comprehensively Governing the Country According to Law for approval or deliberation, or submitted to the Standing Committee of the National People's Congress.

The overall plan for judicial reform issued by the Supreme People's Court shall be reported to the Central Political and Legal Affairs Commission for consent in accordance with procedures; for judicial reform matters involving major institutional or policy adjustments in the political and legal field, they shall be reported to the Central Committee of the Party for consent before implementation in accordance with procedures.

13. After these Provisions are issued, judicial reform matters that are being advanced by local people's courts at any level or special people's courts shall be reported level by level to the Supreme People's Court for review in accordance with these Provisions, unless they have already been submitted in writing to the Supreme People's Court and approved previously.

14. These Provisions shall take effect from the date of issuance.
